2026-11-05          v1.3.0

                    Groups for part of a family, one account in several
                    families, Family Hosting for a new family on a server
                    that charges for it, everything a server stores
                    encrypted under its own key, formulas, diagrams and
                    graphs drawn in the message, emoji and capital letters
                    as you type, animated pictures that move, videos that
                    keep their sound, the same message row on every
                    client, and a new client for Linux.

                    GROUPS. A group is a chat for some of a family's
                    members, with a name and a member list, beside the
                    family chat, which is untouched. Any member starts one
                    — New Group in the New Chat sheet on iPhone, iPad and
                    Android, in the toolbar on the Mac, above the chat
                    list on the web, Windows and Linux — with at least one
                    other person, and becomes its admin. Only the admin
                    renames it, adds and removes people, and deletes it,
                    which asks first and deletes its messages for
                    everyone; anybody can leave, an admin who leaves hands
                    the group to the member who joined it earliest, and
                    the last one out deletes it. Group Info, from the
                    title, shows who is in it. Somebody added later sees
                    the whole history with nothing marked unread. Only its
                    members can see a group at all: everyone else, the
                    family owner included, is told it does not exist.
                    Inside, it is the family chat with fewer people —
                    sender names, threads, polls, mentions of its members,
                    stickers, voice and video messages, formulas, and @ai,
                    which reads only that group. It has no calls, no
                    "seen" ticks and no daily greeting, and its pushes are
                    titled "Group — Sender". A name is 1 to 64 characters
                    and a family has at most 50 groups. Leaving the family
                    leaves every group in it, and coming back does not
                    restore them.

                    SEVERAL FAMILIES. One account — one login, name,
                    picture, birthday, set of blocks and assistant
                    agreement — can now belong to more than one family:
                    somebody in their parents' family starts their own and
                    keeps both. Each family keeps its own chat, groups,
                    board, sticker pack, assistant chat, settings and
                    reports, and nobody is told which other families
                    anybody is in. There are three doors, all inside the
                    app: New Family…, in Settings, while you own no
                    family; Join Another Family…, in Settings, with an
                    invite code; and an owner's Invite…, which offers
                    members of their other families, who accept or decline
                    (at most 50 pending a family). The apps show one
                    family at a time; the family's name at the top of the
                    chats — a menu in the sidebar's header on the Mac —
                    switches between them and shows each one's unread
                    count, and an account in one family sees no switcher.
                    A one-to-one chat belongs to the two people and is
                    listed in every family they share. Leaving or being
                    removed is per family, and the seven-day clock for an
                    account in no family starts only when it leaves the
                    last one. An owner can reset a member's password only
                    if they own every family that member is in. At most 5
                    families an account by default.

                    FAMILY HOSTING. A server may now charge for a NEW
                    family. With the operator's switch on, starting one
                    needs its owner's Family Hosting subscription, monthly
                    or yearly, sold in the App Store and only by the Apple
                    app — one purchase serves iPhone, iPad and Mac. The
                    app shows it on a Host Your Family screen between the
                    family's name and its creation, with Restore
                    Purchases, and Settings shows when it renews or ends,
                    with Manage Subscription. Members never pay, joining
                    is free everywhere, one subscription covers every
                    family its account owns, and every family that existed
                    before the switch, or is created while it is off,
                    stays free. The web, Windows and Linux say that a new
                    family on such a server is started in the iPhone, iPad
                    or Mac app; Android, under Google Play's payments
                    rules, says only that it cannot be started from this
                    app. When a paid family's subscription ends, every
                    member is told, a banner over its chats names the day,
                    and fourteen days later — warned again the day before
                    — the family is deleted, unless it is renewed, handed
                    to a subscriber or made free by the operator;
                    everything works until then. Leaving a family or
                    deleting the account cancels nothing at Apple, and the
                    Apple app says so. A paid family has limits of its
                    own: 25 members, 10 GiB of files, and a monthly
                    assistant allowance of 2,000,000 tokens, 30 pictures
                    and 120 minutes of transcripts, shown in the family's
                    statistics.

                    STORED ENCRYPTED. What people write, say, show and are
                    — messages, notes, poll options, transcripts, names,
                    usernames, password hashes, birthdays, profile
                    pictures, file names, coordinates, reactions, report
                    excerpts, invite codes, push tokens, and every
                    attachment and preview on disk — is sealed by the
                    server before it reaches PostgreSQL or the disk, under
                    a key for each family, each conversation and each
                    account, all of them wrapped by one server key kept in
                    a file outside the database. A dump, a backup, a
                    snapshot or a stolen disk opens nothing without that
                    file. It protects copies, not the running server: the
                    server still reads what it reads, and whoever runs it
                    can too, so this is not end-to-end encryption.
                    Deleting a family, a conversation or an account
                    destroys its key, and a week later no backup can open
                    it; a single message or file deleted on its own is not
                    covered. Nothing changes on the wire, and no client
                    changes.

                    FORMULAS, DIAGRAMS AND GRAPHS. A message bubble now
                    draws what was markup: maths between $ signs, or $$,
                    \[ and \( forms, and fences tagged math, mermaid, dot
                    (or a Graphviz layout name) and plot. One renderer —
                    KaTeX with mhchem, Mermaid, Graphviz and md's plotter
                    — is bundled byte for byte in every client and fetches
                    nothing: the apps draw in a hidden web view that
                    refuses every network request, the web loads it from
                    its own server on the first such bubble. Mermaid runs
                    strict and KaTeX untrusted, a block that fails or
                    takes over 15 seconds shows its source, prices like
                    "$5 and $10" stay text, and nothing inside code is
                    drawn. A diagram or a graph opens full size with a
                    tap. Previews, quotes, pushes, Copy and Share keep the
                    source, and the assistant is told what a bubble draws.

                    CAPITAL LETTERS. A sentence starts with a capital
                    letter as you type in the composer and the other
                    multi-line fields. Windows and the web on a computer
                    do it themselves; the Mac does it while the system's
                    "Capitalize words automatically" is on; Android now
                    asks the keyboard for it, which most keyboards did not
                    do before, and the web on a phone asks the same.
                    Abbreviations, ellipses and code are left alone, and
                    Backspace straight after puts the small letter back.

                    EMOJI AS YOU TYPE. Emoticons — :) 😊, :D 😃, ;) 😉, :( 😞,
                    <3 ❤️ and the rest of Telegram's set, with and without
                    a nose — and 6,552 :name: shortcodes become emoji the
                    moment the last character goes in, in every prose
                    field on every client. Backspace (Delete on the Mac)
                    straight after puts back what was typed. Times,
                    indices and code are guarded, paste, dictation and
                    autocorrect are left alone, and there is no setting.
                    What is sent is the emoji itself.

                    ANIMATIONS. An animated GIF, WebP or PNG put in a chat
                    is turned by the sending device into a short silent
                    looping video — H.264, at most 720 on the short side,
                    30 frames a second, drawn on white — and shown in a
                    photo's box with a GIF label, playing while on screen.
                    It holds its first frame under Reduce Motion,
                    Android's animations off, Windows' animation effects
                    off and prefers-reduced-motion, and above 8 MiB, until
                    it is tapped. Chat lists, quotes and pushes say "GIF".
                    A still GIF or WebP goes as before, and so does an
                    animated one in the assistant's chat. Safari has no
                    ImageDecoder, so there the picture goes as it did in
                    1.2. Animated stickers now move in the sticker panel
                    and the pack as well as in the chat, and hold still
                    under reduced motion on every client. Fixed on the
                    way: Android's photo picker turned a GIF into a still
                    JPEG, and an animated PNG was flattened everywhere.

                    VIDEOS KEEP THEIR SOUND. A video made smaller before
                    sending could lose its sound on every transcoder: a
                    track the platform could not decode was dropped while
                    the transcode reported success, and of two tracks — a
                    screen recording's app sound and microphone — only the
                    first was kept. Sound tracks are now counted from the
                    file itself, several are mixed into one AAC track, and
                    a silent result from a video with sound counts as a
                    failed transcode, so the original goes instead. On
                    Firefox, which has no AAC encoder, a video that needs
                    a mix goes untouched. On iPhone and iPad a video now
                    plays its sound with the Ring/Silent switch on silent,
                    and on Android a video message whose microphone is
                    taken mid-recording stops into review instead of being
                    sent without sound.

                    NEW LINES YOUR WAY. Return sends everywhere. On the
                    Mac, Return with Shift, Option, Command or Control
                    starts a new line — before, Shift sent, Control
                    inserted a line separator and Command did nothing. On
                    Windows it is Enter with Shift, Ctrl or Alt, and on
                    the web Shift, Ctrl, Alt or Meta. With a hardware
                    keyboard, an iPad or iPhone sends on Return and starts
                    a line on Shift, Option or Control with Return, and
                    Android sends on Enter and starts a line on
                    Shift+Enter. Windows now sends line breaks as LF: a
                    message written in several lines there reached the web
                    as one.

                    THE MESSAGE ROW. The composer is the same on every
                    client: the paperclip alone on the left, and inside
                    the field the sticker button, always, the video button
                    while it is empty, and the paintbrush in the
                    assistant's chat. @ai leaves the row — typing @ lists
                    the assistant first, and the paperclip in the family
                    chat has Ask the Assistant. Everything that stacks
                    over the row stacks in one order: notices, voice
                    messages not sent, the reply or edit banner, staged
                    items, the mention list, the busy line. A thread's
                    composer has the same field and Send. On a wide screen
                    — iPad, Android tablet, Mac, Windows and the web — the
                    conversation is a centred column at most 1024 wide,
                    with bubbles at most 560; on the web the composer
                    still spans the pane.

                    MORE. On iPhone, a call from a member linked to a
                    contact is listed under that contact in Recents, not
                    under Unknown Callers, and follows that contact's
                    block and Focus settings. On the Mac, clicking a
                    notification or the Dock brings forward the window
                    that is already open instead of opening another.
                    Windows records video messages: the recorder 1.2
                    shipped switched off is on. In a one-to-one chat, Show
                    text now works on the other person's recording when
                    that person has agreed to the assistant. A chat with
                    many new messages opens faster, and animated stickers
                    scroll smoothly. Android 17 is asked for its new
                    local-network permission only for a server on the home
                    network or a call on the same Wi-Fi.

                    A CLIENT FOR LINUX. Family Connect for Linux is new: a
                    native GTK 4 and libadwaita app in Rust, with
                    WebKitGTK for the formulas and diagrams and GStreamer
                    for calls and media, the Mac's behaviour, and the
                    Windows client's tested logic ported to it. It runs on
                    Ubuntu 26.04 and Fedora 44 and later, on amd64 and
                    arm64, as a .deb, an .rpm or a portable .tar.gz, with
                    nine localisations. It has the family chat, groups,
                    one-to-one chats, several families, calls, voice and
                    video messages, stickers, polls, the board, formulas
                    and emoji as you type, keeps running behind a top-bar
                    icon, can start at login, keeps its sign-in in the
                    Secret Service and reaches the camera and the location
                    through the desktop portals. It notifies only while it
                    runs — there is no push on Linux — and a notification
                    says who wrote, never what. The .deb installs an
                    AppArmor profile that lets WebKit start its sandbox. A
                    build has no server built in unless
                    FAMILY_CONNECT_DEFAULT_SERVER was set when it was
                    compiled, and asks for an address. It has no spell
                    checking, no share sheet and no way to buy Family
                    Hosting. The release workflow attaches its packages to
                    a tag only once linux/packaging/release-ready exists;
                    ci.yml builds all six on every push.

                    FIXED. On iOS and the Mac, message times lost AM and
                    PM on 12-hour locales. On the web, the microphone was
                    cut off on phones, the field now has a visible
                    placeholder, a phone shows one surface at a time, a
                    thread or the open polls open over the chat between
                    721 and 1024 pixels, and Escape no longer closes a
                    thread with a draft in it. On Android, opening a chat
                    with a shared location and map previews on could
                    crash. Windows has a minimum window width, so the
                    composer can no longer be squeezed to nothing.

                    UPGRADING A SERVER — READ THIS FIRST. A 1.3 server
                    refuses to start without [storage] key_file, and its
                    first start converts everything already stored; after
                    that a 1.2 server cannot read the database, and the
                    only way back is a backup taken before. In order
                    (docs/operations.md, "Encryption at rest"): back up
                    with 1.2; make the key with family-connect storage-key
                    new /etc/family-connect/storage.key, owned by
                    family-connect and mode 0400, and copy it off the box
                    by hand (storage-key show) — losing it loses every
                    family on the server, and it is in no backup,
                    deliberately; install 1.3, the new backup script and
                    the key_file line, and start it; check that the
                    database holds sealed bytes and that a photo opens in
                    the app; run a backup with the new script; then delete
                    every backup from before the upgrade, which holds
                    plaintext for ever. The conversion seals 500 rows a
                    transaction, can be resumed, and vacuums the converted
                    tables; files are sealed in the background after the
                    server is up. The server refuses a key file others can
                    read, a key that is not 32 bytes of base64, and the
                    wrong key, naming the fingerprint it expected.

                    SERVER. Seven migrations, applied at startup: 0055
                    (animation), 0056 (groups), 0057 (several_families),
                    0058 (session_families), 0059 (encryption_at_rest),
                    0060 (sealed, applied only after the conversion) and
                    0061 (paid_families). New configuration: [storage]
                    key_file, required; [families] member_families, which
                    when unset follows registration so an upgrade reopens
                    no closed door, and max_families_per_account = 5, from
                    1 to 100; and [subscriptions], off by default —
                    enabled, bundle_id, product_ids, environments
                    (Production and Sandbox), unpaid_days = 14 (1 to 365),
                    max_members = 25, storage_bytes = 10737418240,
                    ai_tokens_per_month = 2000000, images_per_month = 30
                    and transcript_minutes_per_month = 120, each limit off
                    at 0; the section refuses unknown keys and is checked
                    even while off, so a misspelt switch fails at startup.
                    New commands: storage-key new, show and rotate (with
                    the server stopped; the old key is kept as
                    key_file.old and must be kept a week), storage
                    forget-missing, reports, and families list and
                    families plan <id> free|paid. New routes: the group
                    routes under /chats, every family route also as
                    /families/{id}/…, invitations, PUT /me/subscription,
                    which verifies Apple's signed transaction offline
                    against Apple Root CA – G3, and POST
                    /appstore/notifications for App Store Server
                    Notifications v2, which needs no session and takes up
                    to 64 KiB. GET /me carries the account's families and
                    invitations, and, with subscriptions on, the products
                    and the subscription. The backup script dumps the key
                    table separately and keeps seven days of it. A GIF's
                    push says "GIF", and the assistant is told what a
                    bubble draws.

                    OLD CLIENTS KEEP WORKING. A 1.2 client against a 1.3
                    server lists a group under its title and reads and
                    writes in it with none of the group controls; a 1.2
                    app on iOS or Android keeps a deleted group in its
                    list until it is updated. It sees only the account's
                    first family, and if the account leaves that family
                    while staying in another, the server signs out every
                    session that predates several families, and the app
                    signs in again. It cannot buy, so with subscriptions
                    on it cannot start a family, and it shows that refusal
                    as a generic one, with no banner. It shows an
                    animation as an ordinary video that plays silently in
                    the viewer, a formula or a diagram as its source, and
                    an emoji typed on a 1.3 client as the emoji it is. A
                    1.3 client against a 1.2 server sees one family and
                    offers neither New Family… nor Join Another Family…,
                    reads the missing subscription switch as off, and
                    offers New Group, which the old server refuses.

                    The server, web and Linux crates are 1.3.0, the
                    Windows package 1.3, and the Apple and Android apps
                    1.3.

2026-10-02          v1.2.0

                    A family sticker pack, voice and video messages,
                    videos and sound files brought down to one profile
                    before they leave the device, the Mac in the menu
                    bar, one attachment menu and every string in every
                    language on every client, and an assistant that says
                    when its provider refused something instead of
                    failing as though the network had.

                    STICKERS. Each family now has one sticker pack,
                    any member can add to it, and a sticker button in
                    every composer — the family chat, one-to-one
                    chats, the assistant's chat and a thread — sends
                    one with a single tap and no caption. A sticker is
                    drawn without a bubble, in one fixed box, always
                    from its original bytes, and animated where the
                    platform can: Android needs Android 9 for that and
                    shows the first frame before it, and on Windows a
                    WebP needs the WebP Image Extension. Only WebP and
                    PNG are stored, checked by magic number, at most
                    512 KiB each and 200 to a pack by default. A WebP
                    or PNG within that is taken as given, still or
                    animated; any other still picture is fitted into
                    512×512 by the client with its transparency kept;
                    and an animated picture that cannot go as given —
                    an animated GIF, or an animated PNG over the limit
                    — is refused rather than flattened to one frame.
                    Whoever added a sticker can remove it, and so can
                    the owner, nobody else — so a departed member's
                    stickers stay until the owner decides. A sticker
                    message can be replied to, reacted to, reported
                    and threaded, but not edited. Adding or removing
                    one tells nobody.

                    MEDIA BEFORE UPLOAD. A picked video is brought to
                    one profile before it is sent: MP4 with its index
                    at the front, H.264, at most 720 on the short side
                    and 30 frames a second, about 2 Mbit/s at 720p30,
                    HDR tone-mapped to SDR. Lossless sound files, Ogg
                    where the platform can decode it, and MP3 or AAC
                    above 192 kbit/s become AAC. Four rules decide it,
                    written into the protocol and ported to every
                    client, which CI holds to one set of vectors: a
                    clip already within the profile goes untouched,
                    nothing is ever raised, a transcode that fails
                    sends exactly what 1.1 would have, and a result
                    larger than its source is dropped for the source.
                    There is no setting for it on any client. The web
                    and Windows clients compress video for the first
                    time. On iOS and macOS this reverses 1.1's rule of
                    re-encoding only past 100 MB — the 1.1 export
                    preset is now only the fallback — and on Android
                    every clip outside the profile is transcoded, not
                    only an oversized one. The Windows transcoder has
                    not yet been run on Windows itself: every result
                    is read back and compared with its source frame by
                    frame, and anything that fails goes as the
                    original. Stickers and voice notes are never
                    re-encoded, and the 100 MB ceiling is unchanged.

                    VOICE MESSAGES FROM THE SEND BUTTON. With nothing
                    typed, the Send button is a microphone. A tap — or a
                    click, Enter, a screen reader's activation, ⌥⌘R on
                    the Mac and an iPad keyboard, Ctrl+Shift+R on
                    Windows and Android — starts a hands-free recording;
                    the same button, now an arrow, sends it; Stop keeps
                    it to listen to and add words before sending; Delete
                    discards it, asking first from ten seconds on.
                    Holding the microphone does nothing special, on any
                    platform. Nothing under a second is ever sent, a
                    double tap cannot start or send one, and five
                    minutes stops the recording for review instead of
                    sending it. The paperclip's Record Audio is now
                    Record Voice Message, and the assistant's chat no
                    longer offers it. The right-click menu on the
                    microphone offers both kinds of message on the Mac,
                    Windows and the web.

                    NOTHING RECORDED IS LOST OR SENT BY ACCIDENT. There
                    is no recording during a call. A call, the app going
                    to the background, the screen locking or leaving the
                    chat stops a recording and keeps it as a "Voice
                    message not sent" row, kept with its reply and
                    caption until it is sent or deleted — it never rides
                    along with the next text. This fixes Android leaving
                    the microphone on after Back, and iOS switching off
                    a call's audio when a recording stopped.

                    VOICE MESSAGES LOOK FINISHED. A voice message draws
                    its waveform from the recording itself, sent with it
                    by the recording device, so it shows before anything
                    is downloaded; the bars fill in as it plays, and a
                    tap or drag on them jumps. While it plays it shows
                    the time played and a 1×, 1.5× and 2× speed chip
                    each device remembers. A dot marks one this device
                    has not played yet. Its long-press menu now offers
                    only what fits a recording: reactions, Reply, Show
                    text, Playback speed, Save and Report — no Copy or
                    Edit.

                    VIDEO MESSAGES. A round video, recorded in the app:
                    a square 480×480 clip of at most a minute, drawn as
                    a circle. The video button in an empty message box,
                    Record Video Message in the paperclip, or the
                    microphone's right-click menu opens the camera;
                    nothing is recorded until Record, Record turns into
                    Stop and Stop into Send, the camera goes off at
                    Stop, and nothing is sent unseen. A received circle
                    has no bubble, plays in place with sound on a tap
                    and never by itself, and carries Show text under it.
                    The chat list and notifications say "Video message".
                    It is recorded on iPhone, iPad, Mac and Android —
                    where it needs CameraX 1.5.1, the version
                    Scan.Android uses — and in Chrome and Edge. Safari
                    and Firefox say they cannot record one yet; Safari
                    waits for a trial on a real device. Windows plays
                    circles in its viewer, and its recorder is built but
                    switched off until it has been tried on a Windows
                    machine (win/README.md).

                    VOICE NOTES. The web client records AAC where the
                    browser can encode it, then with the browser's own
                    MP4 recorder, and only then as the mono 16 kHz
                    WAV; 1.1 tried the recorder first. It captures in
                    an AudioWorklet, which ends the holes a busy page
                    used to leave in a note. iOS and macOS record at
                    an explicit 64 kbit/s, and Windows asks for the
                    same rate, or the nearest its encoder offers,
                    where 1.1 took whatever Windows chose.

                    THE ASSISTANT. When the provider refuses
                    something, the reply now says so — "The
                    assistant's provider refused that. Try putting it
                    another way." — on all five clients, where it used
                    to say "Couldn't answer that" like any other
                    failure. ai_error carries reason "refused" only
                    when the provider's own content-filter fields say
                    so, and none of the provider's text reaches the
                    client. A refused picture is not given up on at
                    once: the server asks the text model once to
                    reword the description without names or brands,
                    and where the model wrote the description itself
                    it first tries the member's own words. That costs
                    at most three image requests and one rewrite for a
                    picture, with the rewrite's tokens counted in
                    Family Statistics. A /draw draft, and Draw a
                    backdrop, now carry a hint to describe people and
                    things in general words.

                    BACKDROPS ASK FIRST. Drawing an event backdrop
                    sends the event's title to the assistant's
                    provider, so it now needs the author's assistant
                    consent like anything else sent there: the server
                    answers assistant_consent_required (403) without
                    it, and a 1.2 client asks the question and draws
                    on a yes. A refused backdrop is now
                    picture_refused (400) instead of internal (500). A
                    1.1 client shows both as a failed backdrop. The
                    Apple, Android and Windows clients now wait 120
                    seconds for one, where 1.1 gave up after 15, 20
                    and 100, and the web waits on the browser; a 1.1
                    phone can still give up on a slow or reworded
                    backdrop, and the server stops drawing when the
                    connection closes.

                    TEXT OF VOICE AND VIDEO, ON REQUEST. A Show text
                    button under a voice note, an audio file or a video
                    asks the server for its text, which appears under
                    the player for the member who asked and is kept on
                    that device. A member may always get the text of
                    their own messages, in any chat. Another member's
                    message needs the family owner's new switch, Voice
                    and video as text (off by default), and that
                    member's consent to the assistant, and only in the
                    family chat and its threads — never somebody else's
                    message in a direct chat. The asker needs the
                    assistant consent too. Text made from the server's
                    own copy of a recording is kept and handed to later
                    askers without a second charge; a video, an Ogg file
                    or a recording over 25 MB is sent as a sound track
                    the asking device takes out of it, and that text is
                    never kept or shared. Family Statistics shows
                    recordings turned into text and their length.

                    THE ASSISTANT CAN LOOK THINGS UP. Where the server
                    is set up for it, the assistant can search the web
                    or the news (Brave Search or a SearXNG of the
                    operator's own), check the weather (Open-Meteo, for
                    a place named in the question — never the device's
                    location) and read Wikipedia, when a question needs
                    it. It needs the owner's switch, Can look things up
                    (off by default), and each member's own agreement,
                    given separately from the assistant consent and
                    withdrawn with it. Only the short query or place
                    name the model writes leaves the server — never the
                    conversation, names or photos — and a family-chat
                    answer that may look things up leaves out the words
                    of members who have not agreed. An answer ends with
                    up to three source links and the providers' credits
                    (Powered by Brave, the Wikipedia licence,
                    Open-Meteo); a link the model wrote itself that is
                    not a returned source is taken apart, in the
                    finished answer and while it streams, so no device
                    can be made to fetch it. At most three lookups and
                    two rounds an answer, ten seconds a lookup and 100
                    web searches a family a day by default. Family
                    Statistics counts the web searches.

                    WEATHER IN THE MORNING GREETING. The owner can name
                    up to three places, next to the greeting's time, and
                    the daily greeting then mentions today's forecast
                    for each, for that place's own date, with Open-Meteo
                    credited. Only the place names are sent. A forecast
                    that fails or takes longer than eight seconds is
                    left out and the greeting goes out as usual; a
                    request that fails fast — Open-Meteo answers 503
                    when it is overloaded — is asked once more first.

                    THE MAC IN THE MENU BAR. Closing the main window no
                    longer closes the conversation: the Mac keeps
                    running as an icon in the menu bar, still connected,
                    so messages and calls reach it, and the Dock icon
                    shows only while a window is open. A click on the
                    icon brings the window back; its menu has Open,
                    Settings… and Quit, and the unread count stands
                    beside it. Open at Login starts it in the menu bar
                    with no window. Settings ▸ Menu Bar switches each of
                    these.

                    A SHORTCUT TO THE WINDOW. Control-Option-Command-F
                    on the Mac and Ctrl+Alt+Shift+F on Windows bring the
                    window forward from any app, and put it back when
                    pressed again. Never Ctrl+Alt alone on Windows: that
                    is AltGr, which types "[" on Hungarian and Czech
                    keyboards. Settings says when another app already
                    holds the combination.

                    NOTIFICATIONS ON THE MAC. The Mac's notifications
                    follow the Windows client's rules: who wrote, never
                    what — "New message", "New note" — a notification
                    for a new board note too, and nothing for a blocked
                    member or the assistant's answer to one. Settings ▸
                    Notifications has "Tell me when a message arrives",
                    and says when macOS has the app's notifications
                    turned off or set to None, which made a Mac silent
                    with nothing to say why. Switched off, the Mac also
                    takes itself off the server's push list.

                    EVERY STRING IN EVERY LANGUAGE. The Windows and web
                    clients said 172 things in English whatever the
                    language; all of them are translated now, and a
                    check in CI keeps every client complete. Push
                    notifications are written in the language each
                    phone, tablet or Mac shows the app in, instead of
                    always in English — the server's own words only,
                    never what somebody wrote. The Mac's "mentioned you"
                    was English in every language; it is translated.

                    ONE ATTACHMENT MENU. The paperclip offers the same
                    things in the same order on every client, in three
                    groups: Photo or Video, Camera, File and Paste;
                    Record Voice Message and Record Video Message;
                    Location and Poll. The Mac gains Photo or Video from
                    the Photos library, Windows and the web a
                    picture-and-video picker, Windows a paperclip for
                    its "+" and the web icons; on Android Camera opens
                    Take photo and Take video, and Ask for a picture
                    moves to a paintbrush button in the assistant's
                    chat, as on the other clients. An animated GIF
                    picked from the photo library now goes as the GIF it
                    is.

                    FIXED. On Android, a redraw that failed, was
                    refused or was declined no longer blanks the
                    backdrop already showing in the event; only a
                    picture that landed replaces it. On the Mac, Draw
                    a backdrop reads "Drawing…" and stays disabled
                    while that note's request is out, so a second
                    click sends nothing. On Windows, a video dropped
                    onto the board is refused before anything is
                    transcoded, because the board takes photos.

                    SERVER. One migration, 0048 (sticker_pack),
                    applied at startup as always: the pack table, a
                    pack sequence, and a sticker flag and pack link on
                    attachments. Three new keys. [limits]
                    max_pack_items = 200, at least 1 — 0 is refused,
                    not an off switch. [limits] max_pack_item_bytes =
                    524288, at most max_attachment_bytes, so a server
                    whose max_attachment_bytes was set below 512 KiB
                    refuses to start until this is lowered too. [ai]
                    timeout_secs = 180, at least 10, which was
                    hard-coded before and bounds every assistant call;
                    config.example.toml documents it. WebP is now
                    accepted for any photo, and push notifications say
                    "Sticker" where a photo says "Photo". Provider
                    errors are logged on one line with
                    identifier-shaped fields only — never the
                    provider's message, never the member's words.

                    TRANSCRIPTS AND LOOKUPS ON THE SERVER. Three more
                    migrations, 0049 (transcripts), 0050 (lookups) and
                    0051 (greeting_places). Two new optional config
                    tables. [ai.transcribe] names a speech-to-text
                    deployment: the Azure OpenAI audio/transcriptions
                    request by default (gpt-4o-transcribe or
                    gpt-4o-mini-transcribe), or api = "speech" for Azure
                    Speech's MAI-Transcribe models, with max_bytes up to
                    25 MiB and an optional language hint. [ai.lookups]
                    sets search = "brave" with its key, or "searxng"
                    with its URL, weather and wikipedia, a contact URL
                    for the User-Agent (an email address is refused),
                    and the limits above. Neither is on unless
                    configured, and a server without them sends the
                    assistant exactly the requests it did before. The
                    reference nginx configuration has a new location for
                    transcript requests — a 26 MB body and a 300-second
                    read — that an existing server needs copied in. The
                    refusal codes of Azure's gpt-image models
                    (contentFilter) are now read as refusals, so a
                    refused picture is reworded as any other.

                    VIDEO MESSAGES ON THE SERVER. Two more migrations,
                    0052 (round_video) and 0053 (audio_waveform). A
                    video message is an ordinary H.264/AAC MP4
                    attachment sent with round: true; the server refuses
                    the flag, with a 400 and the upload left unclaimed,
                    unless the message is exactly one square video/mp4
                    of 720 or less a side and a minute or less, with no
                    words, and it cannot be edited. GET /families/mine
                    advertises max_round_video_ms and
                    max_round_video_bytes, which is how a client knows
                    the server has video messages. One more key,
                    [limits] max_round_video_bytes: by default 12 MiB,
                    or max_attachment_bytes where that is lower, and
                    when set it must be from 1 to max_attachment_bytes —
                    no existing configuration is refused. A voice note
                    may carry a 48-level waveform, given on its upload
                    and refused when malformed. Push notifications say
                    "Video message" for one.

                    PUSHES IN THE DEVICE'S LANGUAGE. One more migration,
                    0054 (device_language). POST /devices takes an
                    optional language — absent keeps the stored one,
                    null clears it, a malformed one is stored as none
                    and never refuses the registration — and a push's
                    own words ("Photo", "3 Photos", "New message",
                    "asked to join" and the rest) go out in it, from a
                    table generated from the apps' own catalogue. A
                    device with no language, or one the server has no
                    words for, gets English as before. A Mac is pushed
                    who wrote but never what, whatever
                    include_message_body says, because a desktop banner
                    sits on a shared screen.

                    OLD CLIENTS KEEP WORKING. A 1.1 client against
                    this server draws a sticker as an ordinary photo
                    in a bubble, lists it as "Photo", cannot add or
                    send one, ignores the refusal reason, offers Edit
                    on a sticker the same account sent from a 1.2
                    device (the server answers 400), and shows a
                    backdrop that needs consent, or was refused, as a
                    failure. A 1.2 client against a 1.1 server offers
                    no stickers — the missing pack limits are the
                    feature check — and everything else, preparing
                    media included, works unchanged.

                    An app from before these features asks for no text,
                    is never offered lookups (the server needs the
                    member's agreement, which only a new app can give)
                    and shows a greeting's weather and an answer's
                    sources as the ordinary text they are.

                    An app from before video messages — 1.1, or a 1.2
                    build that predates them — draws a video message as
                    an ordinary square video and plays it in its viewer,
                    ignores a voice note's waveform, and is told 400 if
                    it offers Edit on one. An app with video messages
                    against an older server offers none and records
                    voice as before.

                    An app from before these — any 1.1, or a 1.2 build
                    without them — registers no language and gets its
                    pushes in English, and a Mac on it keeps getting
                    message text in its pushes until it is updated.

                    The server and web crates are 1.2.0, the Windows
                    package 1.2.0.0, and the Apple and Android apps
                    1.2.

2026-09-16          v1.1.0

                    Two new clients, threads and mentions in the
                    chat, a board that holds photos, events and
                    lists, and uploads that finish by themselves.

                    TWO NEW CLIENTS. A Windows client (WinUI 3 on
                    .NET 10, packaged for the Microsoft Store) and a
                    web client (Rust and WebAssembly). Both speak the
                    same protocol to the same server as the Apple and
                    Android apps — chats, attachments, the board,
                    polls, one-to-one calls, and the same nine
                    localisations — so a family can be on four kinds
                    of device and one server.

                    CHAT. Threads: a reply chain opens on its own, so
                    a side conversation stops burying the family
                    chat. Mentions: type @ and pick a member, and the
                    person named is told whether or not they had the
                    chat open. Unread state now agrees across a
                    person's devices. And every poll still waiting
                    for answers is one screen away instead of lost in
                    the scroll.

                    THE BOARD. Photo notes, events with who is going
                    and a hand-off into the reader's own calendar,
                    task lists the family ticks off, mentions inside
                    a note, a chosen font for a text note, and a
                    board that scrolls once it fills up. Photos on it
                    are drawn as photos rather than in a balloon, and
                    a note's text fits the note.

                    ATTACHMENTS. A send stages its files on the
                    device and uploads them in the background, so
                    leaving the screen — or the app — no longer
                    strands a photo or a long video half-sent.

                    THE ASSISTANT. A daily greeting, at a time the
                    family picks and off unless they turn it on. It
                    can be shown members' profile pictures and recent
                    photos from the family chat, each its own switch
                    and each off by default. An assistant chat can no
                    longer be removed. And the part that was
                    documented wrongly, now stated where an operator
                    reads it: an @ai mention IN THE FAMILY CHAT sends
                    recent family conversation with the question —
                    other members' words, their display names and
                    their timestamps — for as long as that family's
                    ai_history is on, which is its default. Only the
                    owner can turn it off, and no member is asked
                    first.

                    MODERATION. The owner's report inbox now says
                    what a reported message carried — "Photo", or a
                    file's own name. A photo sent without a caption
                    has an empty body by design, and "inappropriate"
                    is very often exactly that message, so the row
                    used to be a reason word and two names. Resolving
                    a report twice is no longer an error either: the
                    second answer is the same success as the first,
                    which is what a double tap, a second device and a
                    retry after a timeout all deserve.

                    FIXED. The Windows client lost a day of a family
                    chat. Its catch-up cursor counted the chat list's
                    own trimmed preview as a held message, so it
                    asked the server for everything newer than the
                    newest message there was, got nothing, and never
                    asked for the day in between. The protocol now
                    says what that cursor may count — only what a
                    page or a live frame delivered, taken when the
                    connection opens — and the cache counts nothing
                    else. Also: images on the board are no longer
                    cropped at the wrong height, the web client fits
                    a tall picture in both directions, and on the Mac
                    the change-password dialog stays inside its
                    window while the board can add photos and events
                    at all.

                    SERVER. Ten migrations (0036 to 0045), applied at
                    startup as always: threads, message and note
                    mentions, photo notes, board events, note task
                    lists, note fonts, the assistant's greeting and
                    face switches, an assistant chat that cannot be
                    deleted, and a report that carries what the
                    reported message held.

                    The server and web crates are 1.1.0, the Windows
                    package 1.1.0.0, and the Apple and Android apps
                    1.1.

2026-08-31          v1.0.0

                    First release, and the first accurate entry in
                    this file: the v0.1.0 note below was written at
                    the initial commit and never touched again, so
                    it describes a much smaller product than the one
                    that shipped. Everything here is in addition to
                    it.

                    MESSAGES. Replies, editing, emoji reactions,
                    read markers, typing indicators, unread counts
                    and mentions. Photos, videos, voice notes, files
                    and locations — sent alone or as an album of up
                    to ten — with per-family deduplication, so the
                    same picture sent twice is stored once. Polls. A
                    family board of sticky notes. Birthdays. A
                    per-family language.

                    CALLS. One-to-one voice and video between family
                    members: peer-to-peer WebRTC, with the server
                    passing offers, answers and ICE candidates and
                    never media. They ring like real calls — CallKit
                    on iOS, Telecom on Android — and an optional
                    TURN relay covers networks that will not carry a
                    direct connection. Video can be turned off
                    separately from voice, because a relay sized for
                    voice is not sized for video.

                    PUSH NOTIFICATIONS ACTUALLY DELIVER. v0.1.0
                    collected device tokens and logged what it would
                    have sent; this release ships working APNs (with
                    PushKit VoIP for incoming calls) and FCM
                    transports. Note the default: [push]
                    include_message_body = true puts the message
                    text into the notification, which means Apple or
                    Google see it. Set it to false if that is not
                    the trade you want.

                    AN OPTIONAL ASSISTANT. Off unless the operator
                    fills in [ai]. Each member gets one private
                    assistant chat that no other member can read,
                    and the assistant can also be mentioned by name
                    in the family chat. What leaves the server is
                    enumerated in docs/protocol.md and nothing else
                    does — but it does leave the server, to
                    whichever provider the operator configured.

                    SAFETY AND MODERATION. Any member can block
                    another, silently, without the blocked member
                    being told. Any member can report a member or a
                    specific message to the family owner. Owners can
                    reset a member's password, choose whether an
                    invite code joins immediately or needs approval,
                    close the family entirely, and cap its size.
                    Ownership passes on rather than trapping an
                    owner who wants to leave.

                    ACCOUNTS AND HISTORY. Permanent account
                    deletion, profile pictures, family statistics,
                    and a retention sweep that deletes messages and
                    their attachments past a configurable age.

                    APPS. A native macOS app alongside iOS, from one
                    universal build (iOS 17+, macOS 14+), plus the
                    Android app (Android 8+).

                    SERVER. Thirty migrations, applied at startup.
                    Rate limiting at nginx on the two
                    unauthenticated argon2 endpoints, a bound on
                    concurrent password hashes, cgroup memory
                    limits, and a free-space floor that refuses
                    uploads before PostgreSQL runs out of room. A
                    backup script and timer, and a tested restore
                    procedure, in docs/operations.md.

                    The server binary is 1.0.0. It reported 0.1.0
                    throughout all of the above.

2026-08-19          v0.1.0

                    Initial version. Self-hosted family chat: Rust
                    server (Axum + PostgreSQL, REST + WebSocket API,
                    opaque session tokens, families with invite codes
                    and owner approval, family-wide chat plus 1:1
                    member chats, push-notification hooks without a
                    transport yet), SwiftUI iOS client (iOS 17+) and
                    Jetpack Compose Android client (Android 8+), both
                    with offline history caches, optimistic sending
                    with retry, reconnect resync, unread counts, read
                    markers and typing indicators.
